","corresponding":null,"first":"Robert R.","key":"dunnrobertrpau","last":"Dunn","name":"Dunn, Robert R.","rank":41,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"20","corresponding":null,"first":"Nathan J.","key":"sandersnathanjpau","last":"Sanders","name":"Sanders, Nathan J.","rank":42,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Weiwei","key":"liuweiweipau","last":"Liu","name":"Liu, Weiwei","rank":43,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"21","corresponding":null,"first":"Lukas","key":"schraderlukaspau","last":"Schrader","name":"Schrader, Lukas","rank":44,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jacobus J.","key":"boomsmajacobusjpau","last":"Boomsma","name":"Boomsma, Jacobus J.","rank":45,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Guojie","key":"zhangguojiepau","last":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Guojie","rank":46,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["40527318"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.cell.2025.05.030","issn":["00928674","10974172"],"issn_org":["00928674"],"issue":"18","journal":"Cell","publisher":"Cell Press","status":"p","title":"Cell","volume":"188"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105008570750"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-887a691e-b446-45f4-ba3e-42c5bb2ce469"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Adaptive radiation and social evolution of the ants","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-827753","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Background Macrolide maintenance therapy (MMT) has demonstrated notable efficacy in reducing exacerbation in patients with bronchiectasis, which is a major risk factor for cardiovascular events. However, a comprehensive assessment of the cardiovascular benefits and safety profile of MMT in this population is lacking. Methods This territory-wide cohort study analysed patients diagnosed with bronchiectasis in Hong Kong between 2001 and 2018. Patients were classified as MMT receivers or macrolide non-receivers based on the administration of MMT. Propensity score (PS) matching was employed for confounding factors adjustment. The primary outcome of interest was major adverse cardiovascular events (MACE), a composite of cardiovascular death, myocardial infarction and stroke. The safety outcome was the occurrence of ventricular arrhythmias or sudden cardiac death. Cox proportional hazard regression analysis was utilised to compare the incidence of outcomes across the two groups. Results A total of 22 895 patients with bronchiectasis were identified. Following 1:2 PS matching, the final cohort consisted of 3137 individuals, with 1123 MMT receivers and 2014 macrolide non-receivers. MMT administration was associated with a significantly reduced risk of MACE (16.38 versus 24.11 events per 1000 person-years; hazard ratio (HR) 0.68, 95% CI 0.52–0.90). Importantly, the use of MMT was not associated with elevated risk of ventricular arrhythmias or sudden cardiac death (7.17 versus 7.67 events per 1000 person-years; HR 0.93, 95% CI 0.60–1.44). Conclusions The administration of MMT in patients with bronchiectasis was associated with a significant reduction in the risk of MACE, without any evidence suggesting an increased risk of severe arrhythmia-related adverse events.","author_count":16,"contributors":["Guo, Ran","Wat, Dennis","Lam, Steven Ho Man","Bucci, Tommaso","Tsang, Christopher Tze-Wei","Cai, An-Ping","Chan, Yap-Hang","Ren, Qing-Wen","Huang, Jia-Yi","Zhang, Jing-Nan","Gu, Wen-Li","Zhu, Ching-Yan","Hung, Yik-Ming","Frost, Freddy","Lip, Gregory Y H","Yiu, Kai-Hang"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-770116"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Yiu, Kai-Hang"],"country":["China","Hong Kong","Italy","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Guangdong Provincial People's Hospital","Liverpool Centre for Cardiovascular Science","Liverpool Heart and Chest Hospital NHS Foundation Trust","Liverpool John Moores University","Southern Medical University","The University of Hong Kong","The University of Hong Kong-Shenzhen Hospital","University of Liverpool","University of Rome La Sapienza"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"cardiovascular benefits and safety profile of macrolide maintenance therapy in patients with bronchiectasis","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1183/13993003.01574-2024","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["We thank the Hong Kong Hospital Authority for granting access to the data. This study was supported by the Sanming Project of Medicine in Shenzhen, China (SZSM201911020) and supported by HKU-SZH Fund for Shenzhen Key Medical Discipline (SZXK2020081). Funding information for this article has been deposited with the Crossref Funder Registry."],"id":"S01-000.00-770116","identifier":[{"id":"39603670","type":"pmid"},{"id":"86000757429","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-3dd9f4fd-7f3c-4f4a-9f76-33f0a44431dc"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-DB6213869D6BF98F664FF933D64E1D39"],"oa_link":[{"a