{"abstract":"Purpose: Nocturnal urine volume and bladder reservoir function are key pathogenic factors behind monosymptomatic nocturnal enuresis (MNE). We investigated the predictive value of these together with other demographic and clinical variables for response to first-line treatments in children with MNE. Materials and Methods: A randomized, controlled, international, multicenter study was conducted in 324 treatment-na€ıve children (6-14 years old) with primary MNE. The children were randomized to treatment with or without prior consideration of voiding diaries. In the group where treatment choice was based on voiding diaries, children with nocturnal polyuria and normal maximum voided volume (MVV) received desmopressin (dDAVP) treatment, and children with reduced MVV and no nocturnal polyuria received an enuresis alarm. In the other group, treatment with dDAVP or alarm was randomly allocated. Results: A total of 281 children (72% males) were qualified for statistical analysis. The change of responding to treatment was 21% higher in children where treatment was individualized compared to children where treatment was randomly selected (risk ratio [ 1.21 [1.02-1.45], P [ .032). In children with reduced MVV and no nocturnal polyuria (35% of all children), individualized treatment was associated with a 46% improvement in response compared to random treatment selection (risk ratio [ 1.46 [1.14-1.87], P [ .003). Furthermore, we developed a clinically relevant prediction model for response to dDAVP treatment (receiver operating characteristic curve 0.85). Conclusions: The present study demonstrates that treatment selection based on voiding diaries improves response to first-line treatment, particularly in specific subtypes. Information from voiding diaries together with clinical and demographic information provides the basis for predicting response. Clinical Trial Registration No.: NCT03389412.","author_count":18,"contributors":["Jørgensen, Cecilie Siggaard","Dossche, Lien","Zhai, Rongqun","Maternik, Michal","Kamperis, Konstantinos","Breinbjerg, Anders","Karamaria, Sevasti","Thorsteinsson, Kristina","Borg, Britt","Wang, Yihe","Li, Shuai","Raes, Ann","Wei, Lu","Żurowska, Aleksandra","Hagstrøm, Søren","Walle, Johan Vande","Guo, Wen Jian","Rittig, Søren"],"cuid":["S02-099.30-751575"],"filters":{"added":"2024-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Asia, East","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Jørgensen, Cecilie Siggaard"],"country":["Belgium","China","Germany","Poland","Switzerland"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","European Reference Network for Rare Kidney Diseases","Ferring Pharmaceuticals","First Affiliated Hospital of Zhengzhou University","Ghent University Hospital","Medical University of Gdansk","Medical University of Gdańsk","The First Affiliated Hospital of Zhengzhou University","Xinyang Central Hospital"],"keyword":["Desmopressin","Monosymptomatic nocturnal enuresis","Predictive factors","Voiding diaries","alarm"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-NDR"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-NDR","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"development of a novel prediction tool for response to first line treatments of monosymptomatic nocturnal enuresis a randomized controlled international multicenter study drychild","src":["aau","au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"http://www.scopus.com/inward/record.url?scp=85200409852&partnerID=8YFLogxK","version":"Other Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Funding/Support: This study is supported by grants from Knud and Edith Eriksen\\u2019s Memorial Fund, the A.P. M\\u00F8ller Foundation for the Advancement of Medical Science, Dagmar Marshall\\u2019s Fund, Grosserer L.F. Foght\\u2019s Fund, the Frimodt-Heineke Fund, and Familien Hede Nielsen\\u2019s Fund. Conflict of Interest Disclosures: Drs Kamperis, Vande Walle, and Rittig reported receiving honoraria from Ferring Pharmaceuticals for teaching activities. Dr Borg reports being currently employed at Ferring Pharmaceuticals under the program Industrial PhD, which is partly funded by Innovation Fund Denmark."],"id":"S02-099.30-751575","identifier":[{"id":"38950376","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85200409852","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-6cb9f91b-451b-4163-b7c9-b10393338038","au-75c82c34-860c-42e6-a457-5bb812ea8c99"],"keyword":["alarm","desmopressin","monosymptomatic nocturnal enuresis","predictive factors","voiding diaries"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-522D6CA913773B1E9BE806C66D95C98B"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Paediatrics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"be","key":"ghent university hospital","loc":["Belgium","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Ghent University Hospital","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"european reference network for rare kidney diseases","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"European Reference Network for Rare Kidney Diseases","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"cn","key":"the first affiliated hospital of zhengzhou university","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"The First Affiliated Hospital of Zhengzhou University","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"cn","key":"first affiliated hospital of zhengzhou university","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"First Affiliated Hospital of Zhengzhou University","used":1},{"affno":"6","country