","rank":420,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Roman","key":"zagorskyromanpau","last":"Zagorsky","name":"Zagorsky, Roman","rank":421,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Sandor","key":"zoletniksandorpau","last":"Zoletnik","name":"Zoletnik, Sandor","rank":422,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Chijin","key":"xiaochijinpau","last":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Chijin","rank":423,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"B.","key":"madsenbpau","last":"Madsen","locid":"240b6b8f-eb88-4547-8752-d9471e4bca4d","name":"Madsen, B.","orcid":"0000-0001-5716-467X","rank":424,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"M.","key":"salewskimirkopau","last":"Salewski","locid":"38100e3a-6385-47d3-aa2d-6fc5608a8649","name":"Salewski, Mirko","name_org":"Salewski, M.","orcid":"0000-0002-3699-679X","rank":425,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Eugenio","key":"schustereugeniopau","last":"Schuster","name":"Schuster, Eugenio","rank":426,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1088/1741-4326/ad4270","issn":["00295515","10185577"],"issn_org":["00295515"],"issue":"11","journal":"Nuclear Fusion","paperid":"112013","publisher":"IOP Publishing","status":"p","title":"Nuclear Fusion","volume":"64"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85202803876"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-0451b63b-fa9a-4237-8fad-d63338a10129"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Overview of recent experimental results on the EAST Tokamak","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-774915","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Sediment plays a pivotal role in deep-sea ecosystems by providing habitats for a diverse range of microorganisms and facilitates the cycling processes of carbon, sulfur and nitrogen. Beyond the normal seafloor (NS), distinctive geographical features such as cold seeps (CS) and hydrothermal vent (HV) are recognized as life oases harboring highly diverse microbial communities. A global atlas of microorganisms can reveal the notable association between geological processes and microbial colonization. However, a comprehensive understanding of the systematic comparison of microbial communities in sediments across various deep-sea regions worldwide and their contributions to Earth's elemental cycles remains limited. Analyzing metagenomic data from 163 deep-sea sediment samples across 73 locations worldwide revealed that microbial communities in CS sediments exhibited the highest richness and diversity, followed by HV sediments, with NS sediments showing the lowest diversity. The NS sediments were predominantly inhabited by Nitrosopumilaceae, a type of ammonia-oxidizing archaea (AOA). In contrast, CSs and HVs were dominated by ANME-1, a family of anaerobic methane-oxidizing archaea (ANME), and Desulfofervidaceae, a family of sulfate-reducing bacteria (SRB), respectively. Microbial networks were established for each ecosystem to analyze the relationships and interactions among different microorganisms. Additionally, we analyzed the metabolic patterns of microbial communities in different deep-sea sediments. Despite variations in carbon fixation pathways in ecosystems with different oxygen concentrations, carbon metabolism remains the predominant biogeochemical cycle in deep-sea sediments. Benthic ecosystems exhibit distinct microbial potentials for sulfate reduction, both assimilatory and dissimilatory sulfate reduction (ASR and DSR), in response to different environmental conditions. The presence of nitrogen-fixing microorganisms in CS sediments may influence the global nitrogen balance. In this study, the significant differences in the taxonomic composition and functional potential of microbial communities inhabiting various deep-sea environments were investigated. Our findings emphasize the importance of conducting comparative studies on ecosystems to reveal the complex interrelationships between marine sediments and global biogeochemical cycles.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Lu, Rui","Li, Denghui","Guo, Yang","Cui, Zhen","Wei, Zhanfei","Fan, Guangyi","Zhang, Weijia","Wang, Yinzhao","Gu, Ying","Han, Mo","Liu, Shanshan","Meng, Liang"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-777187"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East"],"country":["China"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","BGI","CAS - Institute of Deep-Sea Science and Engineering","CAS - Institute of Oceanology","Chinese Academy of Fishery Sciences","MGI Tech Co., Ltd. (Biz)","Shanghai Jiao Tong University"],"keyword":["Cold seep","Hydrothermal vent","Marine sediment","Metabolic potential","Metagenomics"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"comparative metagenomics highlights the habitat related diversity in taxonomic composition and metabolic potential of deep sea sediment microbiota","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.heliyon.2024.e39055","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-777187","identifier":[{"id":"PMC11616513","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"39634420","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85209120636","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-24aa345b-549b-4d4a-9253-38e0e77a2bff"],"keyword":["Cold seep","Hydrothermal vent","Marine sediment","Metabolic potential","Metagenomics"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-FCEEF6668652AAD8C9F28E723E0B4C19"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.heliyon.2024.e39055","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/413217106/1_s2.0_S2405844024150864_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"cn","key":"bgi","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"BGI","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"cn","key":"cas institute of oceanology","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"CAS - Institute of Oceanology","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"cn","key":"chinese academy of fishery sciences","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Chinese Academy of Fishery Sciences","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"cn","key":"cas institute of deep sea science and engineering","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"CAS - Institute of Deep-Sea Science and Engineering","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"cn","key":"shanghai jiao tong university","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Shanghai Jiao Tong University","used":1},{"affno":"6","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Biology, Genome Research and Molecular Bio Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"7","country":"cn","key":"mgi tech co ltd","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"MGI Tech Co., Ltd. (Biz)","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Rui","key":"lupau","last":"Lu","name":"Lu, Rui","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Denghui","key":"lipau","last":"Li","name":"Li, Denghui","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Yang","key":"guopau","last":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yang","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Zhen","key":"cuipau","last":"Cui","name":"Cui, Zhen","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","first":"Zhanfei","key":"weipau","last":"Wei","name":"Wei, Zhanfei","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Guangyi","key":"fanpau","last":"Fan","name":"Fan, Guangyi","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"Weijia","key":"zhangpau","last":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Weijia","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","first":"Yinzhao","key":"wangpau","last":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yinzhao","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Ying","key":"gupau","last":"Gu","name":"Gu, Ying","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 6","first":"Mo","key":"hanpau","last":"Han","locid":"40fd89c1-9808-423f-9021-ab3911a46e33","name":"Han, Mo","orcid":"0000-0002-4404-297X","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 4 7","first":"Shanshan","key":"liupau","last":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shanshan","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 4","first":"Liang","key":"mengpau","last":"Meng","name":"Meng, Liang","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["39634420"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.heliyon.2024.e39055","issn":["24058440"],"issue":"22","journal":"Heliyon","paperid":"e39055","status":"p","title":"Heliyon","volume":"10"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85209120636"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-24aa345b-549b-4d4a-9253-38e0e77a2bff"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Comparative metagenomics highlights the habitat-related diversity in taxonomic composition and metabolic potential of deep-sea sediment microbiota","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-777187","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Background: The rumen microbiome plays an essential role in maintaining ruminants’ growth and performance even under extreme environmental conditions, however, which factors influence rumen microbiome stability when ruminants are reared in such habitats throughout the year is unclear. Hence, the rumen microbiome of yak (less domesticated) and cattle (domesticated) reared on the Qinghai-Tibetan Plateau through the year were assessed to evaluate temporal changes in their composition, function, and stability. Results: Rumen fermentation characteristics and pH significantly shifted across seasons in both cattle and yak, but the patterns differed between the two ruminant species. Ruminal enzyme activity varied with season, and production of xylanase and cellulase was greater in yak compared to cattle in both fall and winter. The rumen bacterial community varied with season in both yak and cattle, with higher alpha diversity and similarity (beta diversity) in yak than cattle. The diversity indices of eukaryotic community did not change with season in both ruminant species, but higher similarity was observed in yak. In addition, the similarity of rumen microbiome functional community was higher in yak than cattle across seasons. Moreover, yak rumen microbiome encoded more genes (GH2 and GH3) related to cellulose and hemicellulose degradation compared to cattle, and a new enzyme family (GH160) gene involved in oligosaccharides was uniquely detected in yak rumen. The season affected microbiome attenuation and buffering values (stability), with higher buffering value in yak rumen microbiome than cattle. Positive correlations between antimicrobial resistance gene (dfrF) and CAZyme family (GH113) and microbiome stability were identified in yak, but such relationship was negatively correlated in cattle. Conclusions: The findings of the potential of cellulose degradation, the relationship between rumen microbial stability and the abundance of functional genes varied differently across seasons and between yak and cattle provide insight into the mechanisms that may underpin their divergent adaptation patterns to the harsh climate of the Qinghai-Tibetan Plateau. These results lay a solid foundation for developing strategies to maintain and improve rumen microbiome stability and dig out the potential candidates for manufacturing lignocellulolytic enzymes in the yak rumen to enhance ruminants’ performance under extreme environmental conditions.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Guo, Wei","Zhou, Mi","Li, Fuyong","Alves Neves, Andre Luis","Ma, Tao","Bi, Sisi","Wang, Weiwei","Long, Ruijun","Guan, Le Luo"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-778829"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Asia, East","OECD"],"country":["Canada","China"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Chinese Academy of Agricultural Sciences","Guizhou University","Lanzhou University","University of Alberta","University of British Columbia","Zhejiang University"],"keyword":["Cattle"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"seasonal stability of the rumen microbiome contributes to the adaptation patterns to extreme environmental conditions in grazing yak and cattle","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12915-024-02035-4","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-778829","identifier":[{"id":"PMC11515522","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"39443951","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85207375096","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-b6b9954c-04c5-4d7d-91e6-3e29338b5fbb"],"keyword":["Cattle","Microbiome functionality","Rumen metagenome","Rumen microbiome stability","Yak"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical 