{"abstract":"AIMS: The clinical correlates and outcomes of asymptomatic atrial fibrillation (AF) in hospitalized patients are largely unknown. We aimed to investigate the clinical correlates and in-hospital outcomes of asymptomatic AF in hospitalized Chinese patients. METHODS AND RESULTS: We conducted a cross-sectional registry study of inpatients with AF enrolled in the Improving Care for Cardiovascular Disease in China-Atrial Fibrillation Project between February 2015 and December 2019. We investigated the clinical characteristics of asymptomatic AF and the association between the clinical correlates and the in-hospital outcomes of asymptomatic AF. Asymptomatic and symptomatic AF were defined according to the European Heart Rhythm Association score. Asymptomatic patients were more commonly males (56.3%) and had more comorbidities such as hypertension (57.4%), diabetes mellitus (18.6%), peripheral artery disease (PAD; 2.3%), coronary artery disease (55.5%), previous history of stroke/transient ischaemic attack (TIA; 17.9%), and myocardial infarction (MI; 5.4%); however, they had less prevalent heart failure (9.6%) or left ventricular ejection fractions ≤40% (7.3%). Asymptomatic patients were more often hospitalized with a non-AF diagnosis as the main diagnosis and were more commonly first diagnosed with AF (23.9%) and long-standing persistent/permanent AF (17.0%). The independent determinants of asymptomatic presentation were male sex, long-standing persistent AF/permanent AF, previous history of stroke/TIA, MI, PAD, and previous treatment with anti-platelet drugs. The incidence of in-hospital clinical events such as all-cause death, ischaemic stroke/TIA, and acute coronary syndrome (ACS) was higher in asymptomatic patients than in symptomatic patients, and asymptomatic clinical status was an independent risk factor for in-hospital all-cause death, ischaemic stroke/TIA, and ACS. CONCLUSION: Asymptomatic AF is common among hospitalized patients with AF. Asymptomatic clinical status is associated with male sex, comorbidities, and a higher risk of in-hospital outcomes. The adoption of effective management strategies for patients with AF should not be solely based on clinical symptoms.","author_count":22,"contributors":["Lin, Jing","Wu, Xue-Ying","Long, De-Yong","Jiang, Chen-Xi","Sang, Cai-Hua","Tang, Ri-Bo","Li, Song-Nan","Wang, Wei","Guo, Xue-Yuan","Ning, Man","Sun, Zhao-Qing","Yang, Na","Hao, Yong-Chen","Liu, Jun","Liu, Jing","Du, Xin","Fonarow, Gregg C.","Smith, Sidney C.","Lip, Gregory Y H","Zhao, Dong","Dong, Jian-Zeng","Ma, Chang-Sheng"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-693071"],"filters":{"added":"2023-11","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Asia, East","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corpau":["CCC-AF investigators"],"corresponding_author":["Long, De-Yong"],"country":["China","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Beijing Anzhen Hospital","Capital Medical University","Liverpool Heart and Chest Hospital NHS Foundation Trust","National Clinical Research Center for Cardiovascular Disease","University of California at Los Angeles","University of Liverpool","University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill"],"keyword":["Acute coronary syndrome","Atrial Fibrillation diagnosis","Atrial fibrillation","Brain Ischemia","Cardiovascular Diseases diagnosis","Cross Sectional Studies","Female","Humans","Hypertension","Ischemic Attack Transient epidemiology","Ischemic stroke","Male","Prognosis","Quality Improvement","Risk Factors","Stroke","Stroke diagnosis"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"asymptomatic atrial fibrillation among hospitalized patients clinical correlates and in hospital outcomes in improving care for cardiovascular disease in china atrial fibrillation","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/europace/euad272","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["The CCC-AF project is a collaborative project of the American Heart Association and the Chinese Society of Cardiology. The American Heart Association received funding from Pfizer through an independent grant The authors thank all CCC-AF Project investigators and their patients for contributing to these data. The CCC-AF project is a collaborative project of the American Heart Association and the Chinese Society of Cardiology. The American Heart Association received funding from Pfizer through an independent grant for learning and change and AstraZeneca as a quality improvement initiative. The funders had no role in the design and conduct of the study; collection, management, analysis, and interpretation of the data; preparation, review, or approval of the manuscript; and decision to submit the manuscript for publication."],"id":"S01-000.00-693071","identifier":[{"id":"85173567117","type":"scopus"},{"id":"37712716","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["aau-da00d5d0-8921-430b-9c25-db58fcb49d5b"],"keyword":["Acute coronary syndrome","Atrial Fibrillation/diagnosis","Atrial fibrillation","Brain Ischemia","Cardiovascular Diseases/diagnosis","Cross-Sectional Studies","Female","Humans","Hypertension","Ischemic Attack, Transient/epidemiology","Ischemic Stroke","Male","Prognosis","Quality Improvement","Risk Factors","Stroke","Stroke/diagnosis"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-5ACE39A3D149F7E567B8881A40C14AC7"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/europace/euad272","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/552063140/euad272.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affn