R.","rank":16,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jonathan Z.","key":"longpau","last":"Long","name":"Long, Jonathan Z.","rank":17,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["37141889"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.cmet.2023.04.011","issn":["15504131","19327420"],"issn_org":["15504131"],"issue":"7","journal":"Cell Metabolism","pages":"1261-1279","paperid":"e11","status":"p","title":"Cell Metabolism","volume":"35"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85163155389"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-146eef57-25a0-4b74-916f-ccfeea1e681e"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Organism-wide, cell-type-specific secretome mapping of exercise training in mice","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-677603","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Abstract: Microbiota from mothers is an essential source of microbes in early-life rumen microbiota, but the contribution of microbiota from different maternal sites to the rumen microbiota establishment in neonates needs more data. To fill this gap, we collected samples from the mouth, teat skin, and rumen of lactating yaks and from the rumen of sucking calves concomitantly on seven occasions between days 7 and 180 after birth under grazing conditions. We observed that the eukaryotic communities clustered based on sample sites, except for the protozoal community in the teat skin, with negative correlations between fungal and protozoal diversities in the rumen of calves. Furthermore, fungi in the dam’s mouth, which is the greatest source of the calf’s rumen fungi, accounted for only 0.1%, and the contribution of the dam’s rumen to the calf’s rumen fungi decreased with age and even disappeared after day 60. In contrast, the average contribution of the dam’s rumen protozoa to the calf’s rumen protozoa was 3.7%, and the contributions from the dam’s teat skin (from 0.7 to 2.7%) and mouth (from 0.4 to 3.3%) increased with age. Thus, the divergence in dam-to-calf transmissibility between fungi and protozoa indicates that the foundation of these eukaryotic communities is shaped by different rules. This study provides the first measurements of the maternal contribution to the fungal and protozoal establishment in the rumen of sucking and grazing yak calves in early life, which could be beneficial for future microbiota manipulation in neonatal ruminants. Key points: • Dam to calf transfer of rumen eukaryotes occurs from multiple body sites. • A minor proportion of rumen fungi in calves originated from maternal sites. • The inter-generation transmission between rumen fungi and protozoa differs.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Guo, Wei","Liu, Tingmei","Alves Neves, Andre Luis","Long, Ruijun","Degen, Allan","Zhou, Mi","Chen, Xiang"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-684140"],"filters":{"added":"2023-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Asia, East","Asia, Middle East","OECD"],"country":["Canada","China","Israel"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Ben-Gurion University of the Negev","Guizhou University","Lanzhou University","University of Alberta"],"keyword":["Eukaryotic microbes"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"transmission of fungi and protozoa under grazing conditions from lactating yaks to sucking yak calves in early life","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00253-023-12616-y","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-684140","identifier":[{"id":"37341753","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85162272096","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC10345012","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-78bf6294-4c5f-4569-9580-604beb501080"],"keyword":["Dam-to-infant transmission","Eukaryotic microbes","Maternal contribution","Rumen microbial development"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-276FA7D17B29FF328F2515577E696A88"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00253-023-12616-y","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/365555829/s00253_023_12616_y.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"cn","key":"guizhou university","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Guizhou University","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Veterinary and Animal Sciences, Production, Nutrition and Health"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"cn","key":"lanzhou university","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Lanzhou University","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"il","key":"ben gurion university of the negev","loc":["Israel","Asia, Middle East","OECD"],"name":"Ben-Gurion University of the Negev","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"ca","key":"university of alberta","loc":["Canada","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Alberta","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Wei","key":"guopau","last":"Guo","name":"Guo, Wei","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Tingmei","key":"liupau","last":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tingmei","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"André Luis Alves","key":"nevespau","last":"Neves","locid":"381f5fae-b9b0-439f-b5e2-85888508aabe","name":"Alves Neves, Andre Luis","name_org":"Neves, André Luis Alves","orcid":"0000-0002-2617-6210","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Ruijun","key":"longpau","last":"Long","name":"Long, Ruijun","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"Allan","key":"degenpau","last":"Degen","name":"Degen, Allan","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","first":"Mi","key":"zhoupau","last":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Mi","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Xiang","key":"chenpau","last":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiang","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["37341753"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s00253-023-12616-y","issn":["01757598","14320614"],"issn_org":["01757598"],"journal":"Applied Microbiology and Biotechnology","pages":"4931-4945","status":"p","title":"Applied Microbiology and Biotechnology","volume":"107"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85162272096"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-78bf6294-4c5f-4569-9580-604beb501080"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Transmission of fungi and protozoa under grazing conditions from lactating yaks to sucking yak calves in early life","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-684140","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Background: SARS-CoV-2 remains a world-wide health issue. SARS-CoV-2-specific immunity is induced upon both infection and vaccination. However, defining the long-term immune trajectory, especially after infection, is limited. In this study, we aimed to further the understanding of long-term SARS-CoV-2-specific immune response after infection. Results: We conducted a longitudinal cohort study among 93 SARS-CoV-2 recovered individuals. Immune responses were continuously monitored for up to 20 months after infection. The humoral responses were quantified by Spike- and Nucleocapsid-specific IgG levels. T cell responses to Spike- and non-Spike epitopes were examined using both intercellular cytokine staining (ICS) assay and Activation-Induced marker (AIM) assay with quantification of antigen-specific IFNγ production. During the 20 months follow-up period, Nucleocapsid-specific antibody levels and non-Spike-specific CD4 + and CD8 + T cell frequencies decreased in the blood. However, a majority of participants maintained a durable immune responses 20 months after infection: 59% of the participants were seropositive for Nucleocapsid-specific IgG, and more than 70% had persisting non-Spike-specific T cells. The Spike-specific response initially decreased but as participants were vaccinated against COVID-19, Spike-specific IgG levels and T cell frequencies were boosted reaching similar or higher levels compared to 1 month post-infection. The trajectory of infection-induced SARS-CoV-2-specific immunity decreases, but for the majority of participants it persists beyond 20 months. The T cell response displays a greater durability. Vaccination boosts Spike-specific immune responses to similar or higher levels as seen after primary infection. Conclusions: For most participants, the response persists 20 months after infection, and the cellular response appears to be more long-lived compared to the circulating antibody levels. Vaccination boosts the S-specific response but does not affect the non-S-specific response. Together, these findings support the understanding of immune contraction, and with studies showing the immune levels required for protection, adds to the knowledge of durability of protection against future SARS-CoV-2.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Hvidt, Astrid Korning","Guo, Huaijian","Andersen, Rebecca","Nielsen, Stine Sofie Frank","Vibholm, Line K.","Søgaard, Ole Schmeltz","Schleimann, Mariane Høgsbjerg","Russell, Victoria","Cheung, Angela Man Wei","Paramithiotis, Eustache","Olesen, Rikke","Tolstrup, Martin"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-706691"],"filters":{"added":"2024-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Olesen, Rikke"],"country":["Canada"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Samuel Lunenfeld Research Institute, Mount Sinai Hospital, Toronto"],"keyword":["Antibodies","Antibodies Viral","Antigen specific T cells","Covid 19","Humans","Immunity Cellular","Immunity Humoral","Immunoglobulin G","Infection","Longitudinal Studies","SARS CoV 2","Vaccination","Vaccine"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"long term humoral and cellular immunity after primary sars cov 000000000002 infection a 000000000020 month longitudinal study","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12865-023-00583-y","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-706691","identifier":[{"id":"37974069","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC10652616","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85176546100","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-0acc6d2d-0668-4d4e-8d3b-c5a27933bb27"],"keyword":["Antibodies","Antibodies, Viral","Antigen-specific T cells","COVID-19","Humans","Immune durability","Immunity, Cellular","Immunity, Humoral","Immunoglobulin G","Infection","Longitudinal Studies","SARS-CoV-2","Vaccination","Vaccine"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-99E74C0B7C04EFD122CE1356E792EB08"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s12865-023-00583-y","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Unspecified organisation"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Infectious Diseases"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"ca","key":"samuel lunenfeld research institute mount sinai hospital toronto","loc":["Canada","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Samuel Lunenfeld Research Institute, Mount Sinai Hospital, Toronto","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Astrid Korning","key":"hvidtpau","last":"Hvidt","name":"Hvidt, Astrid Korning","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Huaijian","key":"guopau","last":"Guo","name":"Guo, Huaijian","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Rebecca","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","name":"Andersen, Rebecca","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Stine Sofie Frank","key":"lendepau","last":"Lende","locid":"725719a2-01c0-4e6d-af64-6204e4f0ed35","name":"Nielsen, Stine Sofie Frank","name_org":"Lende, Stine Sofie Frank","orc