Waters, Tim","rank":68,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"39","first":"Peter R.","key":"williamspeterrpau","last":"Williams","name":"Williams, Peter R.","rank":69,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"13","first":"Sen","key":"yangsenpau","last":"Yang","name":"Yang, Sen","rank":70,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"13","first":"Zhu-Heng","key":"yaozhuhengpau","last":"Yao","name":"Yao, Zhu-Heng","rank":71,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"6","first":"Theodora E.","key":"zastrockytheodoraepau","last":"Zastrocky","name":"Zastrocky, Theodora E.","rank":72,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"13","first":"Shuo","key":"zhaishuopau","last":"Zhai","name":"Zhai, Shuo","rank":73,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"41","first":"Ying","key":"zuyingpau","last":"Zu","name":"Zu, Ying","rank":74,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.3847/1538-4357/ac2159","issn":["0004637X","00670049"],"issn_org":["0004637X"],"issue":"2","journal":"Astrophysical Journal","paperid":"151","status":"p","title":"Astrophysical Journal","volume":"922"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85120832752"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-d133e22a-6665-4923-bc0b-e973461f2267"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"AGN STORM 2. I. First results : A Change in the Weather of Mrk 817","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-518429","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Type 2 diabetes (T2D) is a systematic chronic metabolic condition with abnormal sugar metabolism dysfunction, and its complications are the most harmful to human beings and may be life-threatening after long-term durations. Considering the high incidence and severity at late stage, researchers have been focusing on the identification of specific biomarkers and potential drug targets for T2D at the genomic, epigenomic, and transcriptomic levels. Microbes participate in the pathogenesis of multiple metabolic diseases including diabetes. However, the related studies are still non-systematic and lack the functional exploration on identified microbes. To fill this gap between gut microbiome and diabetes study, we first introduced eggNOG database and KEGG ORTHOLOGY (KO) database for orthologous (protein/gene) annotation of microbiota. Two datasets with these annotations were employed, which were analyzed by multiple machine-learning models for identifying significant microbiota biomarkers of T2D. The powerful feature selection method, Max-Relevance and Min-Redundancy (mRMR), was first applied to the datasets, resulting in a feature list for each dataset. Then, the list was fed into the incremental feature selection (IFS), incorporating support vector machine (SVM) as the classification algorithm, to extract essential annotations and build efficient classifiers. This study not only revealed potential pathological factors for diabetes at the microbiome level but also provided us new candidates for drug development against diabetes.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Zhang, Yu-Hang","Guo, Wei","Zeng, Tao","Zhang, ShiQi","Chen, Lei","Gamarra, Margarita","Mansour, Romany F.","Escorcia-Gutierrez, Jose","Huang, Tao","Cai, Yu-Dong"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-519994"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Africa","America, South","Asia, East","OECD"],"country":["China","Colombia","Egypt"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","New Valley Univ, Dept Math, Fac Sci","Shanghai Jiao Tong University","Univ Autonoma Caribe, Elect & Telecommun Engn Program","Univ Costa, Universidad de la Costa, Dept Computat Sci & Elect, CUC","University of Chinese Academy of Sciences"],"keyword":["Disorders","Feature selection","Gut microbiome","MELLITUS","Machine learning","Obesity","Prevalence","Support vector machine","Type 2 diabetes","United States"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Shanghai Maritime Univ, Shanghai Maritime University, Coll Informat Engn"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"identification of microbiota biomarkers with orthologous gene annotation for type 000000000002 diabetes","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2021.711244","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-519994","identifier":[{"id":"34305880","type":"pmid"},{"id":"000675855300001","type":"wos"},{"id":"85111166570","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC8299781","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-d3e03ac3-172c-453b-a5a8-71a46533d5a6"],"keyword":["DISORDERS","MELLITUS","OBESITY","PREVALENCE","UNITED-STATES","feature selection","gut microbiome","machine learning","microbiota biomarkers","support vector machine","type 2 diabetes"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-6CFF4C893DC513C67C6043C6B286101E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2021.711244","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/275058060/Identification_of_Microbiota_Biomarkers.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"cn","key":"shanghai jiao tong university","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Shanghai Jiao Tong University","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"cn","key":"university of chinese academy of sciences","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"University of Chinese Academy of Sciences","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Public Health, Section of Biostatistics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Shanghai Maritime Univ, Shanghai Maritime University, Coll Informat Engn"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"co","key":"univ costa universidad de la costa dept computat sci elect cuc","loc":["Colombia","America, South","OECD"],"name":"Univ Costa, Universidad de la Costa, Dept Computat Sci & Elect, CUC","used":1},{"affno":"6","country":"eg","key":"new valley univ dept math fac sci","loc":["Egypt","Africa"],"name":"New Valley Univ, Dept Math, Fac Sci","used":1},{"affno":"7","country":"co","key":"univ autonoma caribe elect telecommun engn program","loc":["Colombia","America, South","OECD"],"name":"Univ Autonoma Caribe, Elect & Telecommun Engn Program","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Yu-Hang","key":"zhangyuhangpau","last":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yu-Hang","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Wei","key":"guoweipau","last":"Guo","name":"Guo, Wei","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Tao","key":"zengtaopau","last":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Tao","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"ShiQi","key":"zhangshiqipau","last":"Zhang","locid":"852efbea-cb2b-45ce-84bd-656211a2a286","name":"Zhang, ShiQi","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"Lei","key":"chenleipau","last":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lei","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","first":"Margarita","key":"gamarramargaritapau","last":"Gamarra","name":"Gamarra, Margarita","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"6","first":"Romany F.","key":"mansourromanyfpau","last":"Mansour","name":"Mansour, Romany F.","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"7","first":"Jose","key":"escorciagutierrezjosepau","last":"Escorcia-Gutierrez","name":"Escorcia-Gutierrez, Jose","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Tao","key":"huangtaopau","last":"Huang","name":"Huang, Tao","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"Yu-Dong","key":"caiyudongpau","last":"Cai","name":"Cai, Yu-Dong","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["34305880"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3389/fmicb.2021.711244","issn":["1664302X"],"journal":"Frontiers in Microbiology","paperid":"711244","status":"p","title":"Frontiers in Microbiology","volume":"12"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85111166570"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-d3e03ac3-172c-453b-a5a8-71a46533d5a6"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Identification of Microbiota Biomarkers With Orthologous Gene Annotation for Type 2 Diabetes","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-519994","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Increased blood lipid levels are heritable risk factors of cardiovascular disease with varied prevalence worldwide owing to different dietary patterns and medication use1. Despite advances in prevention and treatment, in particular through reducing low-density lipoprotein cholesterol levels2, heart disease remains the leading cause of death worldwide3. Genome-wideassociation studies (GWAS) of blood lipid levels have led to important biological and clinical insights, as well as new drug targets, for cardiovascular disease. However, most previous GWAS4–23 have been conducted in European ancestry populations and may have missed genetic variants that contribute to lipid-level variation in other ancestry groups. These include differences in allele frequencies, effect sizes and linkage-disequilibrium patterns24. Here we conduct a multi-ancestry, genome-wide genetic discovery meta-analysis of lipid levels in approximately 1.65 million individuals, including 350,000 of non-European ancestries. We quantify the gain in studying non-European ancestries and provide evidence to support the expansion of recruitment of additional ancestries, even with relatively small sample sizes. We find that increasing diversity rather than studying additional individuals of European ancestry results in substantial improvements in fine-mapping functional variants and portability of polygenic prediction (evaluated in approximately 295,000 individuals from 7 ancestry groupings). Modest gains in the number of discovered loci and ancestry-specific variants were also achieved. As GWAS expand emphasis beyond the identification of genes and fundamental biology towards the use of genetic variants for preventive and precision medicine25, we anticipate that increased diversity of participants will lead to more accurate and equitable26 application of polygenic scores in clinical practice.","author_count":524,"contributors":["Graham, Sarah E.","Clarke, Shoa L.","Wu, Kuan-Han H.","Kanoni, Stavr