{"abstract":"Several blood protein biomarkers have been associated with prostate cancer risk. However, most studies assessed only a small number of biomarkers and/or included a small sample size. To identify novel protein biomarkers of prostate cancer risk, we studied 79,194 cases and 61,112 controls of European ancestry, included in the PRACTICAL/ELLIPSE consortia, using genetic instruments of protein quantitative trait loci for 1,478 plasma proteins. A total of 31 proteins were associated with prostate cancer risk including proteins encoded by GSTP1, whose methylation level was shown previously to be associated with prostate cancer risk, and MSMB, SPINT2, IGF2R, and CTSS, which were previously implicated as potential target genes of prostate cancer risk variants identified in genome-wide association studies. A total of 18 proteins inversely correlated and 13 positively correlated with prostate cancer risk. For 28 of the identified proteins, gene somatic changes of short indels, splice site, nonsense, or missense mutations were detected in patients with prostate cancer in The Cancer Genome Atlas. Pathway enrichment analysis showed that relevant genes were significantly enriched in cancer-related pathways. In conclusion, this study identifies 31 candidates of protein biomarkers for prostate cancer risk and provides new insights into the biology and genetics of prostate tumorigenesis. SIGNIFICANCE: Integration of genomics and proteomics data identifies biomarkers associated with prostate cancer risk.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Wu, Lang","Shu, Xiang","Bao, Jiandong","Guo, Xingyi","Kote-Jarai, Zsofia","Haiman, Christopher A","Eeles, Rosalind A","Zheng, Wei"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-220959"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corpau":["PRACTICAL, CRUK, BPC3, CAPS, PEGASUS Consortia"],"country":["United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Institute of Cancer Research","University of Southern California","Vanderbilt University"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"analysis of over 000000000140 000000000000 european descendants identifies genetically predicted blood protein biomarkers associated with prostate cancer risk","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6744971/pdf/nihms-1535686.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-220959","identifier":[{"id":"85072233973","type":"scopus"},{"id":"31337649","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC6744971","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["au-a0d02ba3-38d1-437a-86f3-fd7df79eeb8a"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-EE6B3EF3A16F818EC3BC6ABAA60C5A5A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6744971/pdf/nihms-1535686.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"vanderbilt university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Vanderbilt University","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"institute of cancer research","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Institute of Cancer Research","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"university of southern california","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Southern California","used":1},{"affno":4,"key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Molecular Medicine (MOMA)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU"},{"affno":5,"key":"practical cruk bpc3 caps pegasus consortia","name":"PRACTICAL, CRUK, BPC3, CAPS, PEGASUS Consortia","role":"oau"}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lang","key":"wupau","last":"Wu","name":"Wu, Lang","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Xiang","key":"shupau","last":"Shu","name":"Shu, Xiang","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jiandong","key":"baopau","last":"Bao","name":"Bao, Jiandong","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Xingyi","key":"guopau","last":"Guo","name":"Guo, Xingyi","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Zsofia","key":"kotejaraipau","last":"Kote-Jarai","name":"Kote-Jarai, Zsofia","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Christopher A","key":"haimanpau","last":"Haiman","name":"Haiman, Christopher A","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Rosalind A","key":"eelespau","last":"Eeles","name":"Eeles, Rosalind A","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Wei","key":"zhengpau","last":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Wei","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["31337649"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1158/0008-5472.can-18-3997","issn":["00085472","05766656"],"issn_org":["00085472"],"issue":"18","journal":"Cancer Research","pages":"4592-4598","publisher":"American Association for Cancer Research Inc.","status":"p","title":"Cancer Research","volume":"79"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-a0d02ba3-38d1-437a-86f3-fd7df79eeb8a"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Analysis of Over 140,000 European Descendants Identifies Genetically Predicted Blood Protein Biomarkers Associated with Prostate Cancer Risk","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-220959","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Temperature increases in spring can advance vegetation green-up date (GUD) due to the increased heat accumulations. However, temperature increases in winter will delay the GUD due to the postponed fulfilment of chilling requirements. Such contrasting impacts of temperature changes in spring and winter on the GUD have been documented from many ecosystems in the Northern Hemisphere. However, the combined effects of temperature variations in winter and spring on the GUD remained unclear, especially in the cold Tibetan Plateau. To advance our understanding of how alpine grassland GUD responds to climate warming, this study evaluated 14 years of field GUD observations of sedge species Kobresia humilis on the Tibetan Plateau. The results showed that January and March-April were two critical periods in determining the long-term GUD variations. The minimum temperatures (T min ) during these two critical periods played the dominant roles in controlling the GUD. Increases in the T min significantly shifting the GUD by −3.9 days °C −1 and 1.7 days °C −1 in the spring and winter, respectively. Moreover, T min in March-April played a more important role in determining the GUD than did T min in January. In addition, the difference in minimum temperature (ΔT min ) between these two critical periods of January and March-April might be a novel indicator for vegetation GUD. This study provides novel insights into the differential impacts of minimum temperatures in winter and spring on the GUD and how they should be explicitly considered to better understand the effects of climate change on vegetation phenology.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Li, Xiaoting","Guo, Wei","Chen, Ji","Ni, Xiangnan","Wei, Xiaoyi"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-230013"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East"],"corresponding_author":["Guo, Wei"],"country":["China"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Northwestern Polytechnical University Xian","Xi'an Jiaotong University"],"keyword":["Alpine grassland","Green up date","Temperature","Tibetan Plateau"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"responses of vegetation green up date to temperature variation in alpine grassland on the tibetan plateau","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85065649830","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-230013","identifier":[{"id":"85065649830","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-b0776814-d441-450e-bca3-7330f9e57b9c"],"keyword":["Alpine grassland","Green-up date","Kobresia humilis","Temperature","Tibetan Plateau"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-35A62FC4DFD17F5F5498260B3CA56C5A"],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"cn","key":"xi an jiaotong university","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Xi'an Jiaotong University","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Technical Sciences, Department of Agroecology, Department of Agroecology - Climate and Water"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"cn","key":"northwestern polytechnical university xian","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Northwestern Polytechnical University Xian","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Xiaoting","key":"lipau","last":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaoting","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Wei","key":"guopau","last":"Guo","name":"Guo, Wei","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Ji","key":"chenpau","last":"Chen","locid":"05f23b9e-16c0-478d-840b-18a8d914c393","name":"Chen, Ji","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Xiangnan","key":"nipau","last":"Ni","name":"Ni, Xiangnan","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Xiaoyi","key":"weipau","last":"Wei","name":"Wei, Xiaoyi","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.ecolind.2019.05.003","issn":["1470160X","18727034"],"issn_org":["1470160X"],"journal":"Ecological Indicators","pages":"390-397","publisher":"Elsevier BV","publisher_org":"Elsevier B.V.","status":"p","title":"Ecological Indicators","volume":"104"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85065649830"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-b0776814-d441-450e-bca3-7330f9e57b9c"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Responses of vegetation green-up date to temperature variation in alpine grassland on the Tibetan Plateau","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-230013","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Hypobaric hypoxia (HH) exposure can cause serious brain injury as well as life-threatening cerebral edema in severe cases. Previous studies on the mechanisms of HH-induced brain injury have been conducted primarily using non-primate animal models that are genetically distant to humans, thus hindering the development of disease treatment. Here, we report that cynomolgus monkeys ( Macacafascicularis) exposed to acute HH developed human-like HH syndrome involving severe brain injury and abnormal behavior. Transcriptome profiling of white blood cells and brain tissue from monkeys exposed to increasing altitude revealed the central role of the HIF-1 and other novel signaling pathways, such as the vitamin D receptor (VDR) signaling pathway, in co-regulating HH-induced inflammation processes. We also observed profound transcriptomic alterations in brains after exposure to acute HH, including the activation of angiogenesis and impairment of aerobic respiration and protein folding processes, which likely underlie the pathological effects of HH-induced brain injury. Administration of progesterone (PROG) and steroid neuroprotectant 5α-androst-3β,5,6β-triol (TRIOL) significantly attenuated brain injuries and rescued the transcriptomic changes induced by acute HH. Functional investigation of the affected genes suggested that these two neuroprotectants protect the brain by targeting different pathways, with PROG enhancing erythropoiesis and TRIOL suppressing glutamate-induced excitotoxicity. Thus, this study advances our understanding of the pathology induced by acute HH and provides potential compounds for the development of neuroprotectant drugs for therapeutic treatment.","author_count":27,"contributors":["Zhang, Pei","Chen, Jie-Si","Li, Qi-Ye","Sheng, Long-Xiang","Gao, Yi-Xing","Lu, Bing-Zheng","Zhu, Wen-Bo","Zhan, Xiao-Yu","Li, Yuan","Yuan, Zhi-Bing","Xu, Gang","Qiu, Bitao","Yan, Min","Guo, Chun-Xue","Wang, You-Qiong","Huang, Yi-Jun","Zhang, Jing-Xia","Liu, Fu-Yu","Tang, Zhong-Wei","Lin, Sui-Zhen","Cooper, David N","Yang, Huan-Ming","Wang, Jian","Gao, Yu-Qi","Yin, Wei","Zhang, Guojie","Yan, Guang-Mei"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-512151"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Asia, East"],"country":["China"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","BGI"],"keyword":["Brain injury","Cynomolgus monkeys","Gene Regulatory Networks"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"neuroprotectants attenuate hypobaric hypoxia induced brain injuries in cynomolgus monkeys","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"djr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.24272/j.issn.2095-8137.2020.012","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-512151","identifier":[{"id":"31840949","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC6956719","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85077790760","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-c6e67e69-bc97-4bf6-8c25-bac243ee2ecc"],"keyword":["Acute hypobaric hypoxia","Brain injury","Cynomolgus monkeys","Gene regulatory networks","Neuroprotectant"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-991C805D7AB535DD3E4A8B4EF483B028"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.24272/j.issn.2095-8137.2020.012","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/248820569/Neuroprotectants_attenuate_hypobaric_hypoxia_induced_brain_injuries_in_cynomolgus_monkeys_version_of_record_.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-K