{"abstract":"As a main destination for recycling, reuse and disposal of solid waste in the global circular economy, China has recently issued a new regulation on its imports, banning 24 types of solid waste in 4 categories, including waste plastics, unsorted scrap papers, discarded textile materials, and vanadium slags. Bans on additional types of solid waste will take effect soon. Here we discuss the possible profound effects of such policy changes on the global circular economy of solid waste. Recycling industries in developed countries will face challenges in the short run, due to their limited capacity and past reliance on exporting, but also opportunities in the long run. Furthermore, developing countries currently without stringent environmental regulations will likely become the new “pollution haven” of solid waste from developed countries and even emerging economies such as China itself. To truly reap the benefits from China's new policies which are originally designed for environmental sustainability and social justice, the global community needs to develop appropriate policy framework to prevent the unintended consequences.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Qu, Shen","Guo, Yuhua","Ma, Zijie","Chen, Wei Qiang","Liu, Jianguo","Liu, Gang","Wang, Yutao","Xu, Ming"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-607634"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Asia, East","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Xu, Ming"],"country":["China","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Central Iron & Steel Research Institute","Chinese Academy of Sciences","Fudan University","Tsinghua University","University of Michigan"],"keyword":["China","Circular economy","Waste trade"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"implications of china s foreign waste ban on the global circular economy","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85061202903","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-607634","identifier":[{"id":"85061202903","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-781eba3c-385d-4dd1-9bec-5f67089a516e"],"keyword":["China","Circular economy","Foreign waste ban","Waste trade"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-7BF71E172C952A48F9F4752E8F8370A8"],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"university of michigan","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Michigan","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"cn","key":"central iron steel research institute","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Central Iron & Steel Research Institute","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"cn","key":"chinese academy of sciences","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Chinese Academy of Sciences","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"cn","key":"tsinghua university","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Tsinghua University","used":1},{"affno":"5","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Engineering, Department of Green Technology (IGT)","SDU, The Faculty of Engineering, Department of Green Technology (IGT), SDU Life Cycle Engineering"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"6","country":"cn","key":"fudan university","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"Fudan University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Shen","key":"qushenpau","last":"Qu","name":"Qu, Shen","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Yuhua","key":"guoyuhuapau","last":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yuhua","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Zijie","key":"mazijiepau","last":"Ma","name":"Ma, Zijie","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Wei Qiang","key":"chenweiqiangpau","last":"Chen","name":"Chen, Wei Qiang","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Jianguo","key":"liujianguopau","last":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jianguo","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Gang","key":"liugangpau","last":"Liu","locid":"7c85e391-8559-40ee-9ede-79ab00cb8a8a","name":"Liu, Gang","orcid":"0000-0002-7613-1985","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"6","corresponding":null,"first":"Yutao","key":"wangyutaopau","last":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yutao","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ming","key":"xumingpau","last":"Xu","name":"Xu, Ming","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.resconrec.2019.01.004","issn":["09213449"],"journal":"Resources, Conservation and Recycling","pages":"252-255","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Resources, Conservation and Recycling","volume":"144"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85061202903"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-781eba3c-385d-4dd1-9bec-5f67089a516e"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Implications of China's foreign waste ban on the global circular economy","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-607634","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Body-fat distribution is a risk factor for adverse cardiovascular health consequences. We analyzed the association of body-fat distribution, assessed by waist-to-hip ratio adjusted for body mass index, with 228,985 predicted coding and splice site variants available on exome arrays in up to 344,369 individuals from five major ancestries (discovery) and 132,177 European-ancestry individuals (validation). We identified 15 common (minor allele frequency, MAF ≥5%) and nine low-frequency or rare (MAF <5%) coding novel variants. Pathway/gene set enrichment analyses identified lipid particle, adiponectin, abnormal white adipose tissue physiology and bone development and morphology as important contributors to fat distribution, while cross-trait associations highlight cardiometabolic traits. In functional follow-up analyses, specifically in Drosophila RNAi-knockdowns, we observed a significant increase in the total body triglyceride levels for two genes (DNAH10 and PLXND1). We implicate novel genes in fat distribution, stressing the importance of interrogating low-frequency and protein-coding variants.","author_count":281,"contributors":["Justice, Anne E","Karaderi, Tugce","Highland, Heather M","Young, Kristin L","Graff, Mariaelisa","Lu, Yingchang","Turcot, Valérie","Auer, Paul L","Fine, Rebecca S","Guo, Xiuqing","Schurmann, Claudia","Lempradl, Adelheid","Marouli, Eirini","Mahajan, Anubha","Winkler, Thomas W","Locke, Adam E","Medina-Gomez, Carolina","Esko, Tõnu","Vedantam, Sailaja","Giri, Ayush","Lo, Ken Sin","Alfred, Tamuno","Mudgal, Poorva","Ng, Maggie C Y","Heard-Costa, Nancy L","Feitosa, Mary F","Manning, Alisa K","Willems, Sara M","Sivapalaratnam, Suthesh","Abecasis, Goncalo","Alam, Dewan S","Allison, Matthew","Amouyel, Philippe","Arzumanyan, Zorayr","Balkau, Beverley","Bastarache, Lisa","Bergmann, Sven","Bielak, Lawrence F","Blüher, Matthias","Boehnke, Michael","Boeing, Heiner","Boerwinkle, Eric","Böger, Carsten A","Bork-Jensen, Jette","Bottinger, Erwin P