{"abstract":"Sufficiently large depletion region for photocarrier generation and separation is a key factor for two-dimensional material optoelectronic devices, but only a few device configurations have been explored for a deterministic control over the space charge region area in graphene with convincing scalability. Here we investigate a graphene-silicon p-i-n photodiode defined in a foundry processed planar photonic crystal waveguide structure, achieving visible—near-infrared, zero-bias, and ultrafast photodetection. Graphene is electrically contacting to the wide intrinsic region of silicon and extended to the p an n doped region, functioning as the primary photocarrier conducting channel for electronic gain. Graphene significantly improves the device speed through ultrafast out-of-plane interfacial carrier transfer and the following in-plane built-in electric field assisted carrier collection. More than 50 dB converted signal-to-noise ratio at 40 GHz has been demonstrated under zero bias voltage, the quantum efficiency could be further amplified by hot carrier gain on graphene-i Si interface and avalanche process on graphene-doped Si interface. With the device architecture fully defined by nanomanufactured substrate, this work demonstrates post-fabrication-free two-dimensional material active silicon photonic devices.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Li, Tiantian","Mao, Dun","Petrone, Nick W.","Grassi, Robert","Hu, Hao","Ding, Yunhong","Huang, Zhihong","Lo, Guo Qiang","Hone, James C.","Low, Tony","Wong, Chee Wei","Gu, Tingyi"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-350713"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Asia, South","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Gu, Tingyi"],"country":["Singapore","United States"],"glorg":["Agency for Science, Technology and Research, Singapore","All Global Affiliations","Columbia University","Hewlett-Packard","University of California at Los Angeles","University of Delaware","University of Minnesota Twin Cities"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"spatially controlled electrostatic doping in graphene p i n junction for hybrid silicon photodiode","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41699-018-0080-4","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-350713","identifier":[{"id":"85070850953","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-9ab75b05-841c-442a-ac5e-943095a74cc9"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-53F13D5CA65D9EEF4A4253E6013A4C40"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/162041834/s41699_018_0080_4.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41699-018-0080-4","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"university of delaware","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Delaware","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"columbia university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Columbia University","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"university of minnesota twin cities","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Minnesota Twin Cities","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Photonics Engineering","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Photonics Engineering, Ultra-fast Optical Communication"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"us","key":"hewlett packard","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Hewlett-Packard","used":1},{"affno":"6","country":"sg","key":"agency for science technology and research singapore","loc":["Singapore","Asia, South"],"name":"Agency for Science, Technology and Research, Singapore","used":1},{"affno":"7","country":"us","key":"university of california at los angeles","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of California at Los Angeles","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Tiantian","key":"lipau","last":"Li","name":"Li, Tiantian","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Dun","key":"maopau","last":"Mao","name":"Mao, Dun","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Nick W.","key":"petronepau","last":"Petrone","name":"Petrone, Nick W.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Robert","key":"grassipau","last":"Grassi","name":"Grassi, Robert","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Hao","key":"hupau","last":"Hu","locid":"ac686760-c537-4304-a9e0-57398f5ebd5f","name":"Hu, Hao","orcid":"0000-0002-8859-0986","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Yunhong","key":"dingpau","last":"Ding","locid":"3b5fb4a4-4890-4c4b-acbc-b335b8ff68c6","name":"Ding, Yunhong","orcid":"0000-0002-6823-4722","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","corresponding":null,"first":"Zhihong","key":"huangpau","last":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zhihong","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"6","corresponding":null,"first":"Guo Qiang","key":"lopau","last":"Lo","name":"Lo, Guo Qiang","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"James C.","key":"honepau","last":"Hone","name":"Hone, James C.","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Tony","key":"lowpau","last":"Low","name":"Low, Tony","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"7","corresponding":null,"first":"Chee Wei","key":"wongpau","last":"Wong","name":"Wong, Chee Wei","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Tingyi","key":"gupau","last":"Gu","name":"Gu, Tingyi","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41699-018-0080-4","issn":["23977132"],"issue":"1","journal":"npj 2D Materials and Applications","paperid":"36","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group","status":"p","title":"npj 2D Materials and Applications","volume":"2"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85070850953"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-9ab75b05-841c-442a-ac5e-943095a74cc9"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Spatially controlled electrostatic doping in graphene p-i-n junction for hybrid silicon photodiode","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-350713","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Prostate cancer is a polygenic disease with a large heritable component. A number of common, low-penetrance prostate cancer risk loci have been identified through GWAS. Here we apply the Bayesian multivariate variable selection algorithm JAM to fine-map 84 prostate cancer susceptibility loci, using summary data from a large European ancestry meta-analysis. We observe evidence for multiple independent signals at 12 regions and 99 risk signals overall. Only 15 original GWAS tag SNPs remain among the catalogue of candidate variants identified; the remainder are replaced by more likely candidates. Biological annotation of our credible set of variants indicates significant enrichment within promoter and enhancer elements, and transcription factor-binding sites, including AR, ERG and FOXA1. In 40 regions at least one variant is colocalised with an eQTL in prostate cancer tissue. The refined set of candidate variants substantially increase the proportion of familial relative risk explained by these known susceptibility regions, which highlights the importance of fine-mapping studies and has implications for clinical risk profiling.","author_count":307,"contributors":["Dadaev, Tokhir","Saunders, Edward J.","Newcombe, Paul J.","Anokian, Ezequiel","Leongamornlert, Daniel A.","Brook, Mark N.","Cieza-Borrella, Clara","Mijuskovic, Martina","Wakerell, Sarah","Olama, Ali Amin Al","Schumacher, Fredrick R.","Berndt, Sonja I.","Benlloch, Sara","Ahmed, Mahbubl","Goh, Chee","Sheng, Xin","Zhang, Zhuo","Muir, Kenneth","Govindasami, Koveela","Lophatananon, Artitaya","Stevens, Victoria L.","Gapstur, Susan M.","Carter, Brian D.","Tangen, Catherine M.","Goodman, Phyllis","Thompson, Ian M.","Batra, Jyotsna","Chambers, Suzanne","Moya, Leire","Clements, Judith","Horvath, Lisa","Tilley, Wayne","Risbridger, Gail","Gronberg, Henrik","Aly, Markus","Nordström, Tobias","Pharoah, Paul","Pashayan, Nora","Schleutker, Johanna","Tammela, Teuvo L.J.","Sipeky, Csilla","Auvinen, Anssi","Albanes,